      <h2 id="channels">Ranking the channels</h2>
      <p>Baijiu distribution outside China is uneven. In some cities — Vancouver, Los Angeles, Singapore, London, Sydney — there are specialist importers with deep stock and staff who know the category. In others, there are three dusty bottles in the back of an Asian grocery and a lot of marketplace listings.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Risk</th><th>Best for</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Licensed spirits specialist / Asian liquor store</td><td>Low</td><td>Everything; the default choice</td><td>Storage conditions; ask how long it has sat in sunlight</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Established online spirits retailer</td><td>Low–medium</td><td>Price comparison, wider range</td><td>Shipping damage; check the returns policy before ordering</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Duty-free at Chinese airports</td><td>Low</td><td>Standard bottles at good prices, travel-exclusive editions</td><td>Purchase limits at destination; no recourse after you leave</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Chinese supermarket with a liquor aisle</td><td>Medium</td><td>Everyday bottles, entry-level brands</td><td>Poor storage, no staff knowledge, occasional grey imports</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Restaurant list</td><td>Medium</td><td>Trying before buying; convivial occasions</td><td>3–4× retail markup; verify the bottle is opened at your table</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Marketplace / private reseller</td><td>High</td><td>Nothing a beginner should need</td><td>Counterfeits, refills, unknown storage, no recourse</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>The practical rule: if you cannot identify who imported the bottle and who is legally responsible for selling it, you are not buying a product — you are buying a story.</p>

      <h2 id="label">Reading a Chinese label</h2>
      <p>You do not need Chinese to evaluate a bottle. Six fields carry almost all the decision-relevant information, and they are all in the same place on every compliant label.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Chinese</th><th>What to look for</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Product type</td><td>产品类型</td><td>固态法白酒 = solid-state (traditional). 液态法 = liquid-fermented. 固液法 = blended.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Aroma type</td><td>香型</td><td>浓香型 strong · 酱香型 sauce · 清香型 light · 米香型 rice · 凤香型 phoenix</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Standard code</td><td>产品标准号</td><td>GB/T 10781.1 strong · 10781.2 light · 26760 sauce · 20821/20822 avoid if seeking traditional</td></tr>
            <tr><td>ABV</td><td>酒精度</td><td>Printed as % vol. 52% vol is standard for strong aroma; 53% for sauce</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Raw materials</td><td>原料</td><td>Sorghum (高粱), wheat (小麦), rice (大米), corn (玉米), barley (大麦), peas (豌豆), water (水)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Producer</td><td>生产商 / 制造商</td><td>Check it matches the brand you think you are buying — group subsidiaries differ</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="callout warning"><strong>The one field that matters most:</strong> 产品标准号 (product standard number). GB/T 10781.x and GB/T 26760 mean solid-state fermentation. GB/T 20821 means liquid-fermented and GB/T 20822 means partially blended. All three are legal; only the first is what most drinkers mean by traditional baijiu. Front labels rarely mention this — the back label always does.</div>

      <h2 id="importer">The importer label</h2>
      <p>In most jurisdictions, a legally imported bottle must carry a locally compliant label applied by the importer: product name in the local language, importer name and address, net volume, ABV, and often an allergen or age statement. This sticker is not decoration. It is the paper trail.</p>
      <p>A bottle with no importer label, or one whose label names a company you cannot look up, has arrived through a channel that did not go through normal import procedures. That does not automatically make it counterfeit — personal imports and cross-border shopping are legal in many places — but it removes your recourse if something is wrong.</p>
      <p>One useful check: the importer label and the original Chinese label should agree on ABV and volume. Mismatches are a strong signal of a repackaged or refilled bottle.</p>

      <h2 id="price">What a fair price looks like</h2>
      <p>Chinese retail prices do not translate directly to overseas prices. Import duty, freight, distributor margin, and retailer margin typically add 30–100% depending on the market, and gift-box editions carry their own premium.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Chinese retail</th><th>Realistic overseas price</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Entry</td><td>¥15–100</td><td>$5–25</td><td>Niulanshan, Hongxing Erguotou, Xifeng</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Premium</td><td>¥200–500</td><td>$30–90</td><td>Fenjiu, Yanghe, Luzhou Laojiao, Gujinggong</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Upper premium</td><td>¥600–1,200</td><td>$80–200</td><td>Wuliangye Puwu, Shuijingfang, Shede</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Prestige</td><td>¥1,499+ (often grey-market above)</td><td>$250–600</td><td>Moutai Flying Fairy</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Collector</td><td>Varies wildly</td><td>$500–5,000+</td><td>Moutai vintage, zodiac, commemorative</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Two pricing signals are worth acting on. A premium bottle priced <strong>well below</strong> the band is a fraud signal, not a bargain — Moutai does not need to be discounted by strangers. A bottle priced <strong>well above</strong> the band is usually a restaurant or a gift-shop markup, and it is worth walking to the next store.</p>

      <h2 id="flags">Red flags</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Price far below market</strong> on a famous premium bottle. The most reliable single indicator of a counterfeit or refill.</li>
        <li><strong>No importer label</strong> on a bottle sold in a retail setting.</li>
        <li><strong>"Official" and "special edition" language</strong> with no exact product name. Vague prestige words are a sales technique, not a specification.</li>
        <li><strong>Vintage claims without documentation.</strong> In baijiu, a year on the box usually marks a release, not the age of the liquid.</li>
        <li><strong>Group-name confusion.</strong> Many products borrow a famous parent name while being a different product line with different liquid. Identify the exact bottle, not the family.</li>
        <li><strong>Seals that have been re-glued</strong>, cap rings that rotate freely, or box shrink-wrap that looks re-applied.</li>
        <li><strong>Seller cannot explain the supply route.</strong> A legitimate importer knows where the pallet came from.</li>
        <li><strong>Pressure to decide quickly.</strong> Scarcity urgency is a sales tactic, and in baijiu it is almost always manufactured.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>For a detailed treatment of the highest-risk category, see <a href="/how-to-spot-fake-maotai/">how to spot fake Maotai</a>.</p>

      <h2 id="gift">Buying as a gift</h2>
      <p>Gift purchasing follows different rules from personal purchasing, because the bottle is a social object. Three principles:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Buy recognition, not novelty.</strong> A recipient should be able to place the bottle instantly. Wuliangye, Yanghe, Gujinggong, and Fenjiu are all instantly readable in China.</li>
        <li><strong>Buy from a channel you could name if asked.</strong> If the recipient asks where it came from, "a licensed importer in Chinatown" is a good answer. "An online seller" is not.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep the receipt.</strong> Not because you expect to return it, but because a gift that can be verified is a better gift.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Also consider the packaging convention: Chinese gifts are usually presented in the box, with both hands, and often with the bottle still sealed and the box unopened. Do not pre-open a gift bottle to check it unless you have a specific concern — buy from a source you trust instead.</p>

      <h2 id="checklist">The 60-second checklist</h2>
      <p>Before money changes hands:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Can I name the exact product line, not just the brand?</li>
        <li>Is there an importer label with a company I can look up?</li>
        <li>Does the back label show a GB/T standard, and do I know what it means?</li>
        <li>Does the price sit inside a plausible band for this bottle?</li>
        <li>Is the seller able to tell me the aroma type and ABV without reading the box?</li>
        <li>Is the seal intact and the cap ring unbroken?</li>
        <li>If this is a gift, would I be comfortable naming the seller?</li>
      </ol>
      <p>If any answer is no, do not buy. There is always another bottle.</p>

      <section class="faq" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
        <h3>Where should I buy baijiu overseas?</h3>
        <p>Start with licensed spirits retailers, established online spirits shops, known importers, and specialist Asian liquor stores. Trust and traceability matter far more than the lowest price, because the price gap between a real and a fake bottle is much larger than the gap between two honest retailers.</p>
        <h3>Are marketplace listings safe?</h3>
        <p>Some are legitimate, but risk is materially higher when seller identity, import route, storage history, and exact product line are unclear. For everyday bottles under $40 the risk is tolerable; for anything above $100 it is not.</p>
        <h3>Can I bring baijiu home from China?</h3>
        <p>Usually yes, within your destination country's personal allowance — commonly 1–2 litres duty-free, with duty above that. Check your own country's rules before flying, keep the bottle in checked baggage with proper padding, and declare what you are carrying.</p>
        <h3>Why is baijiu so much more expensive outside China?</h3>
        <p>Import duty, freight, distributor and retailer margins, and low volume all add up. A bottle that costs ¥300 in China can reasonably retail at $80 abroad. Markups beyond roughly double the converted Chinese price usually reflect channel positioning rather than cost.</p>
        <h3>Is duty-free baijiu a good deal?</h3>
        <p>Often yes for standard bottles, and airport shops in China carry travel-exclusive editions that are genuinely different products. Check the price against a specialist retailer at your destination — duty-free is not automatically cheaper, and the editions are not automatically better.</p>
        <h3>What is the biggest beginner buying mistake?</h3>
        <p>Buying a famous-looking bottle without knowing whether it is authentic, fairly priced, or suitable for the occasion. The second biggest is assuming an expensive bottle must be a good one — in baijiu, price tracks gifting demand at least as much as quality.</p>
